WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.640 --> 00:00:05.620
Sometimes the best comedic actors falter on
SNL, while an athlete or musician can prove

00:00:05.620 --> 00:00:09.800
surprisingly funny, and people you're certain
will bomb find a way to hold their own.

00:00:09.840 --> 00:00:14.600
In celebration of one of television's great
institutions, here are the best and worst

00:00:14.600 --> 00:00:16.380
hosts to ever step on the SNL stage.

00:00:17.080 --> 00:00:21.080
Many people mistakenly believe that Steve
Martin was a cast member on SNL.

00:00:21.280 --> 00:00:25.240
That's both a testament to the impact he's
had on the show and an indication of just

00:00:25.240 --> 00:00:27.020
how popular he is with viewers.

00:00:27.200 --> 00:00:32.300
Martin is the current runner-up to Alec Baldwin
as most frequent host, with 15 episodes to

00:00:32.300 --> 00:00:36.080
his name plus countless cameo appearances
over the past four decades.

00:00:36.530 --> 00:00:40.480
What's perhaps most telling is how frequently
Martin appeared in the show's early years.

00:00:40.480 --> 00:00:46.110
He hosted for the first time on October 23,
1976, and in less than two years he'd already

00:00:46.110 --> 00:00:48.280
joined the illustrious "Five Timers Club."

00:00:48.590 --> 00:00:49.960
"Thanks, Mr. Martin."

00:00:49.960 --> 00:00:53.360
"Oh please, call me 'Mr. Steve Martin.'"

00:00:54.340 --> 00:00:59.230
In those early days he originated the characters
that made him an SNL favorite, from "King

00:00:59.230 --> 00:01:03.100
Tut" and Theodoric of York, to one of the
"Wild and Crazy" Festrunk Brothers.

00:01:03.420 --> 00:01:07.560
And of course we can't forget his very famous
Christmas wish in one of the show's great

00:01:07.560 --> 00:01:08.560
solo sketches.

00:01:09.560 --> 00:01:13.990
Sometimes SNL books hosts who aren't professional
performers but are just people having a pop

00:01:13.990 --> 00:01:14.920
culture moment.

00:01:15.340 --> 00:01:20.180
That can be a recipe for disaster, like when
Donald Trump hosted in 2004 when The Apprentice

00:01:20.180 --> 00:01:25.440
was riding high, and then again in 2015, when
he was in the midst of campaigning for president.

00:01:25.860 --> 00:01:30.439
The result was predictably high ratings, but
not very good comedy, and the show's cast

00:01:30.440 --> 00:01:33.000
wasn't particularly happy to have Trump in
the building.

00:01:33.280 --> 00:01:38.140
Former cast member Taran Killam later described
feeling intense shame over the episode, while

00:01:38.320 --> 00:01:42.500
Pete Davidson commented that Trump was "weird
all week" during rehearsals.

00:01:42.820 --> 00:01:47.399
Regardless of how you feel about him as president,
we can generally agree that Donald Trump was

00:01:47.400 --> 00:01:48.940
not a great SNL host.

00:01:49.820 --> 00:01:55.400
Tom Hanks has hosted SNL nine times since
1985, meaning he's starred in classic sketches

00:01:55.400 --> 00:01:57.120
in four separate decades.

00:01:57.490 --> 00:02:01.900
He's long distinguished himself as a versatile
performer, equally adept at dark dramas and

00:02:01.900 --> 00:02:03.080
screwball comedies.

00:02:03.400 --> 00:02:07.880
In the particular crucible of SNL, he somehow
manages to show a different side of himself

00:02:07.880 --> 00:02:13.060
again and again, proving just how good and
how funny he really is time after time.

00:02:13.580 --> 00:02:18.510
There's something very reassuring about seeing
Hanks on the SNL stage, as "America's Dad"

00:02:18.510 --> 00:02:20.660
gets ready to goof off with the kids for the
night.

00:02:20.960 --> 00:02:25.400
He's had unforgettable chemistry with numerous
cast members in a wide variety of sketches.

00:02:25.620 --> 00:02:29.880
He's gotten huge laughs while checking the
levels on the mics as a roadie for Aerosmith,

00:02:29.890 --> 00:02:34.560
forgetting everything as Mr. Short Term Memory,
and donning a black and orange suit as David

00:02:34.560 --> 00:02:35.560
S. Pumpkins.

00:02:36.320 --> 00:02:38.180
"Any questions?"

00:02:38.980 --> 00:02:42.640
Everyone wants to know the dirt on what happens
behind the scenes at SNL.

00:02:42.980 --> 00:02:46.959
But the show has a reputation as an inviting
space for celebrities to come in and try their

00:02:46.960 --> 00:02:48.180
hand at live comedy.

00:02:48.660 --> 00:02:53.030
So cast members can't necessarily say anything
bad about certain celebrities while they're

00:02:53.030 --> 00:02:53.920
working on the show.

00:02:54.280 --> 00:02:58.879
That means that rude or unpleasant behavior
often evaporates into the ether after a host

00:02:58.880 --> 00:02:59.440
leaves.

00:02:59.740 --> 00:03:04.140
But in some cases, cast members can't stop
talking about a disaster, and that was the

00:03:04.140 --> 00:03:08.980
case with Justin Bieber, who served as both
host and musical guest in 2013.

00:03:09.260 --> 00:03:13.879
Former SNL star Bill Hader, who's never been
the type to gossip about former hosts unless

00:03:13.880 --> 00:03:18.160
they really deserved it, called the Bieber
experience his worst in eight years on the

00:03:18.160 --> 00:03:18.660
show.

00:03:18.940 --> 00:03:20.800
"Yeah, he just was in a bad place."

00:03:20.800 --> 00:03:21.300
"Yeah."

00:03:21.300 --> 00:03:24.470
"Maybe he's in a better place, but then he
was in a very…

00:03:24.640 --> 00:03:25.920
It was rough."

00:03:26.160 --> 00:03:31.100
Hader also noted that Bieber showed up with
a large entourage and was rude to cast members.

00:03:31.180 --> 00:03:35.720
But he was also sympathetic with the then-teenage
superstar's place in the world at the time.

00:03:35.720 --> 00:03:40.730
"He just seemed, like exhausted, or just at
the end of a rope, or whatever.

00:03:40.730 --> 00:03:42.480
He was just so huge."

00:03:43.040 --> 00:03:47.520
Christopher Walken hasn't hosted SNL in a
decade, but in his seven appearances on the

00:03:47.520 --> 00:03:50.500
show, he's made a mark that few other hosts
could ever hope to.

00:03:50.700 --> 00:03:54.480
Not every host with a distinct personality
can fit into SNL's style.

00:03:54.740 --> 00:03:58.460
But others manage to find a way to work their
weirdness right into the show's framework.

00:03:58.660 --> 00:04:01.000
In Walken's case, that's exactly what happened.

00:04:01.440 --> 00:04:06.080
His unique voice, expressive face, and ability
to be extremely weird and extremely funny

00:04:06.080 --> 00:04:12.010
at the same time suit SNL's writers perfectly,
and the result is a series of classic episodes.

00:04:12.010 --> 00:04:17.020
"The Continental," his creepy lothario recurring
character, always produces plenty of laughs.

00:04:17.340 --> 00:04:21.620
And one particular sketch starring Walken
as a record producer introduced an iconic

00:04:21.620 --> 00:04:23.900
phrase into the world's pop culture lexicon.

00:04:24.280 --> 00:04:25.140
"Guess what?

00:04:25.980 --> 00:04:31.040
I got a fever, and the only prescription is
more cowbell."

00:04:32.180 --> 00:04:38.160
Paris Hilton hosted SNL in 2005 when her reality
show The Simple Life and her sex tape scandal

00:04:38.160 --> 00:04:39.840
made her particularly infamous.

00:04:40.060 --> 00:04:44.660
It made sense for SNL to seek her out to pull
in big ratings, but the resulting episode

00:04:44.660 --> 00:04:46.340
was far from the show's best.

00:04:46.700 --> 00:04:51.340
Hilton's comedy chops left plenty to be desired,
and it turns out that behind the scenes she

00:04:51.340 --> 00:04:52.700
was an even bigger headache.

00:04:53.140 --> 00:04:56.020
Then-head writer Tina Fey was definitely not
a fan.

00:04:56.420 --> 00:04:58.800
In an interview with Howard Stern, she called
Hilton,

00:04:58.800 --> 00:05:02.060
"unbelievably dumb and so proud of how dumb
she is."

00:05:02.300 --> 00:05:06.010
She also noted that the heiress once refused
to leave her dressing room after hearing a

00:05:06.010 --> 00:05:10.650
sketch idea revolving around her sex tape,
and she would continually offer pitches that

00:05:10.650 --> 00:05:12.860
mocked various celebrities she didn't like.

